magic: the court has found a new pleasure, and the playhouse galleries are empty on feast days
scene: Tuesday night, a good production, and the house is a third full. {N} stands at the back of the stalls counting the empty seats: four hundred and twelve. Most of the people in the full ones are over seventy.
scene W: A theatre the town pays for owes the whole town a reason to come in, not only the people who always have.
scene U: {N} has the postcode figures: whole streets have never bought a ticket, and some are a ten-minute walk from the door.
scene B: The funders' review is in the spring, and empty seats are the first thing it counts. {N} needs a number to show {boss} and the funders.
scene R: A brilliant show playing to empty seats. {N} wants to go out and drag people in off the street by the arm.
scene G: Audiences come and go like tides. The people in the seats tonight have come for forty years; their grandchildren have not started yet.
scene tribal: At the summer gathering fewer bands have come to hear the tellings than in any year {N} remembers. The young ones sit by the river with songs of their own.
scene magic: The court has taken up the new fashion for mirror-plays in the great houses, and the playhouse's galleries are empty on feast days. {N}, master of the playhouse, counts the bare benches.
- W | open every Tuesday at a price anyone in town can pay | v: universalism | door | tribal: open the tellings to every band, with no gift asked | magic: open the playhouse every Tuesday at a price any apprentice can pay | chance: 75
- U | find out who never comes, and ask them what would bring them | v: achievement, universalism | tribal: go to the bands that stayed away, and ask what they would come for | magic: send runners to the wards that never come, and ask what would bring them | chance: 70
- B | cut the season by two shows, and spend the money on one big name | v: power, achievement | identity | tribal: give fewer tellings, and send for the most famous teller in the valley | magic: cut two plays, and spend the purse on the realm's most famous player | chance: 70
- R | take the show out of the building, and play it in the market square | v: stimulation, self-direction | door | tribal: take the telling to the young ones' own fire by the river | magic: take the play out of the playhouse, and give it from a cart in the market square | chance: 75
- G | take the actors round the schools, and grow the next audience slowly | v: benevolence, tradition | binds | habit | grants: good name in town | tribal: take the tellers round every hearth with children, and grow the next listeners slowly | magic: take the players round the schools and the guild halls, and grow the next crowd slowly | chance: 80
- W>G | give free seats every week to the care homes and the youth clubs | v: benevolence, universalism | mark: helped someone in need | habit | tribal: give the best places at the fire to the old ones and the children of every band | magic: give free benches every week to the almshouses and the apprentices | chance: 90
- U>W | report the free seats as sold in the figures for the funders | v: security, conformity | self_control: - | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: free seats counted as sales in a funding report; backfire: the funders audit the box office and cut the grant | tribal: tell the elders more bands came than did, so the gathering keeps its tellings | magic: enter the free benches as sold in the ledger for the Order | chance: 90
- B>U | bring in a famous touring company to fill the house, and study how they sell | v: achievement | tribal: invite a famous band of tellers to the gathering, and watch how they draw the crowd | magic: bring in a famous travelling company, and study how they fill the galleries | chance: 85
- R>B | stage a show the town will argue over, and let the row sell it | v: stimulation, power | tribal: give a telling so bold the bands will argue over it, and let the quarrel bring them | magic: stage a play so bold the city argues over it, and let the scandal fill the galleries | chance: 50
- G>R | open the bar and the foyer to the town every night, music and all | v: hedonism, benevolence | tribal: keep a fire burning for anyone, every night, with songs and broth | magic: open the playhouse tavern to the town every night, music and all | chance: 70
works: By spring the house is fuller and younger, and those who pay for it are pleased.
works: The new crowd trickles in, then comes in groups, and many of them are young.
fails: The seats stay empty, and those who fund the house count every one.
fails: The new crowd comes once for the novelty, and does not come back.
